WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:06.250
Soft robots might be the next big thing, but
right now, they’re very delicate.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.219
Any small rips or punctures could render these
devices unusable.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:14.240
To combat this, researchers have built soft
robots with a material that,

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:19.240
with a little heat, can reassemble itself after some types
of damage.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.610
The robots get this healing factor from their
construction.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.920
They are made entirely of a synthetic material
called an elastomer,

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.960
short for “elastic polymer.”

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.740
When heated, the molecular bonds that keep
the elastomer together loosen,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.400
and begin to reform back to their original cube-like shape

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:40.420
A little over an hour after being damaged,
the material was almost completely healed,

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:44.670
and 24 hours after cooling, the only evidence
was a small scar.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.300
In testing, this material was adapted to

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.280
a robotic hand, a gripping claw, and an artificial muscle.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.040
Each one represents a standard use for a soft
robot,

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:58.480
and each one was successfully able to heal when heated.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.000
The robots could withstand cuts and stabs,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.620
and achieved nearly-full performance once healed.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.810
Even after two healing cycles, the robots
could still perform well.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.340
The researchers say that these self-healing
elastomers

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.980
could make soft robots more commonplace,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.300
now that their weakness for pointy objects
has been eliminated.

